# Day 04 – Group Policy Objects (GPOs) and Security Baselines

## 🎯 Purpose
Enforce security configurations, standardize desktop environments, and automate administrative tasks across all domain-joined computers and users from a central location.

---

## 🔑 Key Concepts & Terms

* Group Policy Object (GPO): A virtual collection of policy settings used to configure operating systems, applications, and user settings in an Active Directory environment.
* GPMC (Group Policy Management Console): The primary GUI tool (`gpmc.msc`) used by System Administrators to create, edit, and link GPOs.
* Linking: A GPO does nothing until it is "linked" to a specific container in Active Directory, typically an Organizational Unit (OU) or the Domain root.
* Computer Configuration: Policies applied to the physical machine as it boots up, regardless of who logs into it (e.g., Firewall rules, startup scripts).
* User Configuration: Policies applied to the user at the moment they log in, regardless of which computer they are using (e.g., Desktop wallpaper, folder redirection).

---

## 🏗️ Architecture: GPO Inheritance

```text
corp.local (Domain Level)
│
└── _Company (OU Level)  <--- [LINKED: "Company-Security-Baseline" GPO]
    │                         (Rule: Prohibit access to Control Panel)
    │
    ├── IT (Child OU)
    │   └── Admin User        (Inherits Rule: Control Panel Blocked)
    │
    └── HR (Child OU)
        └── Jane Doe          (Inherits Rule: Control Panel Blocked)
```
*Rule of thumb:* Policies flow downstream. A GPO linked at a parent OU automatically applies to all child OUs inside it.

